[{"order":1,"heading":"Asynchronous Programming in .NET: A Deep Dive","type":"heading","text":"Asynchronous Programming in .NET: A Deep Dive"},{"order":2,"subheading":"Introduction","type":"heading","text":"Introduction"},{"order":3,"paragraph":"Asynchronous programming in .NET is built around the Task Model (Task and Task<T>) and the async/await keywords. It enables non-blocking execution, allowing your application to handle high-throughput scenarios like web requests and I/O-bound operations without freezing the calling thread.","type":"paragraph","text":"Asynchronous programming in .NET is built around the Task Model (Task and Task<T>) and the async/await keywords. It enables non-blocking execution, allowing your application to handle high-throughput scenarios like web requests and I/O-bound operations without freezing the calling thread."},{"order":4,"subheading":"Understanding Task and ValueTask","type":"heading","text":"Understanding Task and ValueTask"},{"order":5,"paragraph":"A Task represents an asynchronous operation. While Task is a reference type allocated on the heap, ValueTask is a struct introduced to optimize performance in scenarios where methods frequently complete synchronously, avoiding unnecessary heap allocations.","type":"paragraph","text":"A Task represents an asynchronous operation. While Task is a reference type allocated on the heap, ValueTask is a struct introduced to optimize performance in scenarios where methods frequently complete synchronously, avoiding unnecessary heap allocations."},{"order":6,"code":"public async ValueTask<int> ProcessDataAsync(bool cached)
{
    if (cached)
    {
        return 42; // Synchronous completion without allocation
    }
    return await FetchFromDatabaseAsync(); // Asynchronous completion
}","type":"code","text":"public async ValueTask<int> ProcessDataAsync(bool cached)
{
    if (cached)
    {
        return 42; // Synchronous completion without allocation
    }
    return await FetchFromDatabaseAsync(); // Asynchronous completion
}"},{"order":7,"subheading":"The Role of SynchronizationContext","type":"heading","text":"The Role of SynchronizationContext"},{"order":8,"paragraph":"SynchronizationContext captures the current execution context and marshals continuations back to it. For example, UI frameworks like Windows Forms and WPF use a SynchronizationContext to ensure UI elements are updated on the main UI thread.","type":"paragraph","text":"SynchronizationContext captures the current execution context and marshals continuations back to it. For example, UI frameworks like Windows Forms and WPF use a SynchronizationContext to ensure UI elements are updated on the main UI thread."},{"order":9,"paragraph":"In ASP.NET Core, there is no SynchronizationContext by default, which improves throughput by allowing continuations to run on any available thread pool thread.","type":"paragraph","text":"In ASP.NET Core, there is no SynchronizationContext by default, which improves throughput by allowing continuations to run on any available thread pool thread."},{"order":10,"subheading":"Avoiding Deadlocks with ConfigureAwait","type":"heading","text":"Avoiding Deadlocks with ConfigureAwait"},{"order":11,"paragraph":"When writing library code, you should typically use .ConfigureAwait(false) to avoid capturing the SynchronizationContext. This prevents deadlocks in synchronization-bound environments and reduces context switching overhead.","type":"paragraph","text":"When writing library code, you should typically use .ConfigureAwait(false) to avoid capturing the SynchronizationContext. This prevents deadlocks in synchronization-bound environments and reduces context switching overhead."},{"order":12,"code":"public async Task<string> GetDataAsync()
{
    HttpResponseMessage response = await httpClient.GetAsync("https://api.example.com/data").ConfigureAwait(false);
    return await response.Content.ReadAsStringAsync().ConfigureAwait(false);
}","type":"code","text":"public async Task<string> GetDataAsync()
{
    HttpResponseMessage response = await httpClient.GetAsync("https://api.example.com/data").ConfigureAwait(false);
    return await response.Content.ReadAsStringAsync().ConfigureAwait(false);
}"},{"order":13,"subheading":"Best Practices for Asynchronous Programming","type":"heading","text":"Best Practices for Asynchronous Programming"},{"order":14,"bullet_point":"Avoid async void except for top-level event handlers.","type":"bullet_point","text":"Avoid async void except for top-level event handlers."},{"order":15,"bullet_point":"Use Task.Yield() or Task.Run() carefully to manage CPU-bound vs I/O-bound operations.","type":"bullet_point","text":"Use Task.Yield() or Task.Run() carefully to manage CPU-bound vs I/O-bound operations."},{"order":16,"bullet_point":"Propagate CancellationToken through your async call stack to support cancellation gracefully.","type":"bullet_point","text":"Propagate CancellationToken through your async call stack to support cancellation gracefully."},{"order":17,"bullet_point":"Do not block tasks using .Result or .Wait(), as this can lead to thread pool starvation and deadlocks.","type":"bullet_point","text":"Do not block tasks using .Result or .Wait(), as this can lead to thread pool starvation and deadlocks."}]
